rieve)\u202f—\u202falongside the supporting cast.\n\n- **Transition (Frame\u202f7420):** Stage lights dim, the actors fade into darkness, signaling the end of the live performance and the start of the closing credits.\n\n- **Credits (Frames\u202f7430‑7500):** A series of static black screens with white text scroll through the production’s acknowledgments:  \n\n  1. **CAST** – lists principal roles (Ariel, Eric, Ursula, Sebastian, Scuttle, etc.) and supporting ensemble members (Andrina, Arista, Sailors, etc.).  \n  2. **PRODUCTION TEAM** – credits the director/choreographer, music director, set and costume designers, technical staff, and other crew.  \n  3. **Additional roles** – front‑of‑house, wig & makeup, marketing, student choreographers, stage crew, media, photography, videography, and special thanks.\n\n**Overall Flow:** The segment moves from a live, energetic bow by the full cast to a dimmed stage, then to a clean, text‑only credit roll that details every performer and crew member who contributed to *The Little Mermaid* production at Strathcona Christian Academy.', 'failed_images': []}}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I need to determine the last major event in this Little Mermaid theatrical performance. Based on the VLM query results, I can see that frames 7400-7500 show the final curtain call and credits, which would be the very end of the video. However, I need to identify what the last major story event is before this conclusion.\n\nLet me look at the frames just before the credits to see what the final story scene might be. I\'ll query frames around 7300-7400 to see what happens immediately before the curtain call.\n\n```json\n{\n    "tool": "VLM_QUERY",\n    "frames": ["frames/frame_7300.jpg", "frames/frame_7310.jpg", "frames/frame_7320.jpg", "frames/frame_7330.jpg", "frames/frame_7340.jpg", "frames/frame_7350.jpg", "frames/frame_7360.jpg", "frames/frame_7370.jpg", "frames/frame_7380.jpg", "frames/frame_7390.jpg"],\n    "prompt": "Describe what is h