or.\n\n3. **No Narrative Action**: There is no dialogue, singing, or plot-driven movement. Instead, actors are walking into position, smiling, waving, and taking bows — all hallmarks of a post-performance acknowledgment of the audience.\n\n4. **Final Group Pose**: In Frame 10, the entire cast is lined up across the stage, arms raised or waving, in a celebratory, unified pose — the traditional “final bow” that concludes a show.\n\n5. **Lighting and Atmosphere**: The stage lighting remains bright and celebratory, with spotlights highlighting the cast as they take their bows — consistent with the end of a performance.\n\n---\n\n### **What’s Happening in the Frames:**\n\n- **Frame 1**: The cast begins to assemble on stage. Characters like Scuttle (bird costume) and Prince Eric are visible, walking into position. The stage is lit with blue and green underwater-themed lighting.\n\n- **Frame 2**: More ensemble members join, forming lines. The group is organizing itself for the bow.\n\n- **Frame 3**: The cast is mostly in place. A character in a chef’s hat (possibly Grimsby or a townsfolk) stands near the center. The ensemble is clapping and smiling.\n\n- **Frame 4**: Ursula (in black, with tentacles) and other key characters take their places. The group is now fully assembled, with actors clapping and facing the audience.\n\n- **Frame 5**: Prince Eric walks to center stage, likely to lead the final bow. The cast is arranged symmetrically.\n\n- **Frame 6**: Ariel (in red hair and mermaid-style dress) moves to center stage to join Eric — the leads taking their final bow together.\n\n- **Frame 7**: The full cast is now in place. Ariel and Eric are center stage, surrounded by supporting characters like King Triton, Sebastian, and others.\n\n- **Frame 8**: The cast begins to bow. Ariel and Eric are holding hands, taking a synchronized bow — a classic romantic finale gesture.\n\n- **Frame 9**: They rise from the bow, still holding hands, smiling at the audience. The rest of the 